export const t = {
  "1:א": `What Shulchan Aruch wrote — fire fell, etc., for food for three meals:`,
  "1:ב": `Terumat HaShulchan wrote that likewise permitted to save lechem mishneh for each meal; and it is not comparable to above siman 294 — for here he does no prohibition at all; we are concerned from the side of agitation he will forget and extinguish; and they made an enactment that he save only for three meals; at minimum what is needed for each meal permitted to save (and see s.k. 4); and in drink — all other liquids — all he needs he saves, for there is no fixed amount for drinking; but wine he may not save more than the need of three meals even in a place where most of their drinking is wine — see there:`,
  "1:ג": `Magen Avraham s.k. 1 — some permit. Specifically in another house as is written seif 1 Bach; in a house where the fire is — not more important muktzeh money than permitted things — permitted only food for three meals needed for Shabbat; and the reason — if we do not permit him to save — tell him lest he desecrate Shabbat intentionally; and if we permit him to save — tell him from tircha he will forget it is Shabbat and desecrate Shabbat; therefore better not to permit him to save so he not desecrate inadvertently — more fitting to watch he not come to inadvertence than he not come to intentional — for one who desecrates intentionally is wicked and rebellious and we are not responsible for rebels; and better to save him from error; unlike in a house where there is no fire — there he is not agitated as Shulchan Aruch writes — then we permit to save so he not desecrate intentionally since there is no inadvertence since he is not agitated — fitting to save also the intentional; if so, those who forbid — that is Tur who forbids even in another house — not like Rabbenu Yehuda; and Taz — see there — wrote the opposite:`,
  "2:_": `(s.k. 2) One need not be concerned, etc.; and that which we say one forgot, etc. — tractate Shabbat daf 142b:`,
  "3:א": `(s.k. 3) And some forbid — specifically one for whom it became dark, etc. — this is the view of those who permit as they permitted above siman 266 seif 6 regarding one for whom it became dark on the road with money and no non-Jew and no donkey and no companion — on account of loss they permitted him shevut to carry it less than four amot at a time until he reaches his house; and also permitted him prohibition of moving muktzeh on account of loss; and likewise here on account of concern of loss from coercion or fire — permitted to move muktzeh; and those who forbid hold here it is not comparable to that of siman 266 — for specifically in that of siman 266 they permitted him shevut because a person is agitated over his money and if we do not permit him shevut of moving less than four amot he will carry it directly four amot and more at once until he reaches his house and do a Torah prohibition — therefore they permitted him d'rabbanan prohibition so he not come to Torah prohibition; unlike here in coercion — why permit him d'rabbanan prohibition since there is no concern that if we do not permit him he will do Torah prohibition — from what shall we be concerned? If we are concerned he will take out to reshus harabim to escape the coercers — on the contrary he fears to take them out lest the coercers see the matter and rob him; and this reason suffices for concern of coercers; but concern of loss from fire — this does not suffice that one say he will take out to reshus harabim to escape the fire; and for this one must say like the second reason that he wrote — we have no reshus harabim, as below siman 345 seif 7; and if we permit him muktzeh moving lest he take them out to reshus harabim and come to two d'rabbanan prohibitions — muktzeh moving and carrying out to karmelis — it would be better to permit him one d'rabbanan prohibition — namely to move muktzeh for this; Rabbenu Yehuda wrote we are not permitted to permit d'rabbanan prohibition what we do not find explicitly in Shas; and in Shas we do not find they permitted d'rabbanan prohibition except so he not do Torah prohibition; but so he not do two d'rabbanan prohibitions — we did not find the Sages permitted d'rabbanan prohibition — see there; and if we are concerned in fire lest he extinguish — not according to Tur who holds melacha she'ein tzricha l'gufah exempt even if he extinguishes there is only d'rabbanan prohibition; rather Tur comes to reject the words of those who permit even if he holds like Rambam melacha she'ein tzricha l'gufah liable — nevertheless why concern lest he extinguish since it deals with a house adjacent to fire as Magen Avraham wrote s.k. 1 and there he is not so agitated that we concern lest he extinguish; and see Terumat HaShulchan what he wrote; and Bach ruled like those who permit and wrote also here whether in coercion or fire there is concern — if we do not permit him muktzeh moving he will do Torah prohibition — meaning he will dig in the ground there to escape fire and coercers:`,
  "3:ב": `Forbidden for all to move. Meaning even according to those who permit whom Bach agreed with; and in Shulchan Aruch he wrote only that some say even via a non-Jew forbidden in merchandise — not like those who permit via non-Jew; and as is written above siman 307 seif 19; but via Israelite — there is none who permits; and so siman 308 seif 37; and in the gemara, etc. — the law of siman 308 seif 37 derives from this gemara — and it is in Beitza daf 36b — R' Chiya of Abaye — rain fell on his millstones on Yom Tov and he was concerned they would spoil from rain — for in their days millstones were made of clay — and he asked Rabba what to do and they answered he should bring his bed to the place of the millstones and via the little rain that already fell on the millstones part of the millstones became mud and it would be repugnant in his eyes — and it would be like a graf shel re'i permitted to move on account of its repulsiveness — and he would bring the millstones to a place where rain did not fall; and Abaye challenged him — do we make a graf shel re'i l'chatchila? And Rabba did not answer him; and Tosafos challenged — what is Rabba's view? For Abaye challenged well; and they resolved — in a place of loss they make graf shel re'i l'chatchila; and so below siman 338 seif 8; and see above siman 308 seif 37; and on this Magen Avraham was challenged — since there is loss — why should he make graf shel re'i? He should move it directly; or that in a place of loss also forbidden for an Israelite to move even according to Bach who agreed with those who permit; however the reason is not clear why it was not permitted if rain falls on merchandise or regarding millstones; and what he wrote regarding coercion and fire — why in merchandise and millstones he is not so agitated — if so what does he bring from millstones; and likewise afterward from fit fruits — and who told Magen Avraham to equate agitations to each other — perhaps in millstones and fit fruits there is not so much agitation as in merchandise on which rain falls; and also no hint of this in Ran's words who wrote "and so somewhat in Ran"; rather Magen Avraham's intent is specifically in fire and bandits they permitted since he is agitated — tell him he will do Torah prohibition — for also in bandits tell him he will dig in the ground as Bach wrote; unlike the above merchandise where there is no concern he will do Torah prohibition; and likewise in millstones why moving was not permitted to him; and so explained in Rashba's responsum siman 484 — the questioner asked: merchandise on which rain falls and it is lost, etc. — what is the law to move it on Shabbat or tell a non-Jew to move it? For Rav in Asher HaLevi wrote permitted to hide money from the government on Shabbat; and Rashba answered Ba'al HaTerumah's words are not correct — in fire specifically they permitted one who extinguishes does not lose, etc.; and likewise from this that they compare to R' Chiya of Abaye where dew fell and they permitted only by bringing his bed, etc., and not comparable to one for whom it became dark on the road who gives his pouch to a non-Jew — there they permitted for if you do not permit him to move he will come to do Torah melacha; but here why permit moving? And even to tell a non-Jew they permitted only for the first matter so he not come to d'oraisa prohibition — but in general not, for whoever does not act does not tell a non-Jew to act — end of Rashba's words; it is clear from his words why forbidden in merchandise and millstones since there is no concern he will do Torah prohibition; and so Magen Avraham's intent; and likewise regarding fit fruits the reason is there is no concern he will do Torah prohibition:`,
  "3:ג": `And all Magen Avraham's proof follows this — wherever there is no concern he will do Torah prohibition — even according to those who permit — meaning Ba'al HaTerumah — forbidden for an Israelite to move; only Ba'al HaTerumah holds also in coercion tell him lest he do Torah prohibition as Bach wrote — not like Rashba and Tur; and so somewhat in Ran ch. 24 in tractate Shabbat who first clarified Ba'al HaTerumah's words who permitted as stated; and afterward what rejected Ramban's words and what Rashba challenged Ramban's rejection — only rejected Ba'al HaTerumah's words from another angle — they did not permit in one for whom it became dark on the road, etc., except so he not do Torah prohibition; but on account of coercion and bandits where there is no concern he will do Torah prohibition — we do not permit him, as is written in that responsum; and on this Ran wrote, and this is his language: and this responsum I do not recognize — for as we say here where there is no non-Jew with him and you permitted him, etc., so I do not move four amot at once, etc., here too tell one who bandits come upon him — if you do not permit him, etc., he will not listen to you and will move even four amot at once — and I see no difference between this and that at all — end of his words; it is clear from his words that also what is permitted in coercion is only so he not do Torah prohibition; only it is possible Ran holds in his own view that also in coercion tell him he will do Torah prohibition — therefore fitting to permit him moving; but if there were no concern he will do Torah prohibition — we would not permit moving as Rashba wrote; but Ba'al HaTerumah in his own view possibly holds even where there is no concern he will do Torah prohibition — nevertheless permitted on account of loss of moving; but it is implied from his language he comes to justify Ba'al HaTerumah's view thus — that Rashba's difficulties should not challenge him — it appears Ba'al HaTerumah also holds thus and agrees they did not permit moving except where tell him he will do Torah prohibition; therefore he wrote "and so somewhat" — it is not absolute necessity to say thus; and to exclude Pri Megadim's view who wrote in s.k. 3, and this is his language: and it appears according to Ran he holds to distinguish between a place where he is agitated and where he is not agitated — thus there is no need for this law of Rashba — for here too by lost merchandise he is also not so agitated and fitting to permit even to move by hand — end of his words; and his intent is to what Ran wrote in his conclusion to distinguish according to Ba'al HaTerumah from another angle, and this is his language: for this reason the Sages permitted here regarding one for whom it became dark, etc.; and in fire not permitted (except food for three meals) — because all loss falls suddenly, etc.; and one must hurry its saving — such as in fire like this it is relevant to say — from a person being agitated over his money — if you permit him he will come to extinguish — because the matter is very urgent and his heart is not on it — if you permit him something he too will not give his words to measures and will come to extinguish — for in all such cases there is more concern; and from a person being agitated (and he comes to extinguish) — from where is there concern? A person does not stand, etc. (and he desecrates Shabbat intentionally); but here (in one for whom it became dark) the hour is not so pressing that his heart be agitated over it — and if you permit him he too will save in the manner you permitted him alone; and if not you permit him something he too will not stand himself on his money and save as he needs, etc.; and regarding bandits the matters appear that a person's heart is agitated over them like fire since their saving is urgent, etc.; and if you permit him something he will come to d'oraisa prohibition — end of Ran's words; and from this Pri Megadim learned in the case where he is not so agitated like bandits — Ran agrees moving is permitted; if so in merchandise where he is also not so agitated — moving permitted even via Israelite; and this is not so — for Ran also agrees they do not permit moving except if we do not permit him one can say he will do Torah prohibition; only he holds in a place where he is urgent and greatly agitated it is good there is concern — for if we do not permit him he will desecrate Shabbat in Torah prohibition anyway; against this there is concern — if we permit him via a person being agitated he will forget it is Shabbat and desecrate inadvertently even in what we did not permit him; and there is more concern for this concern that via permitting him moving he will desecrate Shabbat more in Torah prohibition than we concern if we do not permit him he will transgress Torah prohibition (and Bach's view); unlike when he is not so agitated — there is no concern if we permit him moving he will transgress Torah prohibition since he is not agitated; and since there is no such concern — if so we concern if we do not permit him moving since impossible to stand oneself on one's money he will transgress Torah prohibition intentionally since there is no other concern against it; but Ran agrees in a place where there is no concern if we do not permit him moving he will do Torah prohibition — they do not permit moving; and since in merchandise it appears he will do Torah prohibition if we do not permit moving — moving forbidden for all; and as is implied from Ran's words; and so in beginning ch. 5 of Beitza, etc. — we learned one removes fruits (spread on the roof to dry and rain began to fall on them) via a skylight on Yom Tov; and one covers fruits with vessels on account of rain; and Ulla said even airspace of bricks (leftover from building) — even though they are muktzeh permitted to cover on account of rain (for he holds a vessel may be moved even for something that may not be moved); and he challenged — but we learned one covers fruits — it implies specifically fit fruits and not airspace of bricks; and they answered since the beginning taught one removes fruits — the end also teaches fruits; and on this Magen Avraham was challenged — the matter itself is difficult — it should have taught also in the beginning one removes airspace of bricks and covers airspace of bricks with vessels; rather perforce one must say the beginning that teaches one removes — meaning moving by hand forbidden in airspace of bricks that is muktzeh even though there is loss; and the reason likewise there is no concern if we do not permit him he will do Torah prohibition:`,
  "4:_": `(s.k. 4) He saved, etc. — members of his household permitted, etc.; and so Rabbenu Yehuda wrote, etc. — for what was not permitted to him except three meals — the reason since a person is agitated in saving he will forget it is Shabbat and come to extinguish; and since not permitted except food for three meals that each needs on Shabbat — tell him recognition and he will not extinguish; therefore even if one would say what will he eat — nevertheless permitted since in any case he has recognition:`,
  "6:א": `(s.k. 6) Yom Kippur, etc. — it appears to me that even though Yom Kippur does not fall on erev Shabbat or on Sunday, as is written below siman 428 seif 1 — Yom Kippur does not fall on erev Shabbat or on Sunday:`,
  "6:ב": `And he knows he will not find, etc. — and it is as if Yom Kippur falls on erev Shabbat — permitted to save on Thursday; and likewise the reverse teaches us — if Yom Kippur falls on Monday and he knows on Sunday he will not find to buy — nevertheless not permitted to save on Shabbat for Yom Kippur:`,
  "7:א": `(s.k. 7) For Motzei, etc. — on account of distress of the soul; see siman 211 Magen Avraham s.k. 2:`,
  "7:ב": `Meaning they will fast. And after Yom Kippur ends he must still wait until food is prepared for eating:`,
  "10:_": `(s.k. 10) Food, etc. — but more forbidden — to exclude Mahari Weil's view who wrote Ran in his name that others permitted to save even more than three meals — for from this reason not permitted to the homeowner to save more than three meals since a person is agitated over his money — if he saves more than three meals from tircha of saving he will forget and extinguish to save everything; unlike others — good for them if that extent while it burns they are permitted to acquire; but when it is extinguished it is no longer hefker and they will not be permitted to acquire — teaches us that even others may not save more — for in any case if they were permitted to save more they would be preoccupied and from tircha they will forget and extinguish and not put to heart that after extinguishing they will not be permitted to acquire:`,
  "11:א": `(s.k. 11) Since, etc. — but if he did not say, etc. — to exclude Ran's view who holds even if he did not say to you — nevertheless automatically it is hefker immediately like one who saves from the edge of the sea and from the power of the river — for behold it is his since the owners despair since they cannot save — likewise here; but Bach wrote here it is different — presumptively one can say he does not despair — for he himself is not permitted to save more; nevertheless he can say I would have saved via a non-Jew that I would have told all who save should not lose, as is written siman 326:`,
  "11:ב": `And it appears to me without, etc. — I would have found for myself, etc. — meaning according to Bach if he is in a place where no non-Jews live or no non-Jews are found there except him and the rescuer — even if he did not say to you it is hefker; and on this Magen Avraham wrote nevertheless he can say I would have found, etc.:`,
  "13:א": `(s.k. 13) And it is not, etc. — from hefker they acquired — meaning for if they wished they could acquire all the things since it is hefker; only since they know not with his will he declared hefker — they did not wish to acquire; nevertheless for wages they acquired from hefker — not from the law of hire but from the law of acquisition from hefker; for according to known wage measure it is pleasing to the homeowner that it be hefker, as Magen Avraham wrote afterward:`,
  "13:ב": `As is written siman 307 seif 4. One who hires workers to guard seeds does not give sachar Shabbat even though guarding seeds has no melacha prohibition — nevertheless called sachar Shabbat:`,
  "13:ג": `As is written siman 266. One for whom it became dark erev Shabbat on the road with money — on account of loss they permitted him shevut — for if we do not permit the shevut since a person is agitated over his money he will carry it four amot and do Torah prohibition; but if he found a lost object — shevut was not permitted since it has not yet come to his hand he is not agitated and no need he do Torah prohibition — likewise here if the Sages were permitting shevut — namely according to Ben Beteira who holds daf 120 permitted to save even to a courtyard that is not mixed — then specifically for himself permitted on account of loss; and likewise others if they saved for the homeowner's need — permitted even to a courtyard that is not mixed; but if they wish to acquire from hefker for themselves and others — it is like a lost object that has not yet come to hand and shevut was not permitted for their own need — if so l'chatchila not permitted to save and acquire from hefker even for their wages to a courtyard that is not mixed:`,
  "13:ד": `He acquired it. And see Choshen Mishpat siman 208 regarding a transaction done in violation:`,
  "13:ה": `It is in the gemara a chasid, etc. — until now deals with yarei shamayim who does not act beyond the law — but a chasid who acts beyond the law should not take, etc.:`,
  "13:ו": `And Shulchan Gavoah wrote if they returned it, etc. — siman 183 — Reuven sent an agent to receive money from a non-Jew and the non-Jew erred and gave him more — permitted for the agent; and specifically if the agent knew while still in his hand — then the agent acquired it; but if not known to him by mistake until given — Reuven can no longer acquire it — and likewise here:`,
  "13:ז": `(And see in Sefer Ateret Rosheinu) and in siman 122 who wrote one who gave permission to one to collect his debt for him — if he wrote in the power of attorney "all who are exempted from judgment upon me return" — there are two practical differences — if he did not write thus the authorized agent could acquire the money received from the borrower for himself; unlike when he wrote thus he is not permitted to withhold for himself; another practical difference — via this writing he must return to the agent all expenses he spent on this; and Semag challenged — all the more if he did not write thus he must return his expenses; for if the agent wishes he can acquire the entire debt — all the more he can acquire the money received for the expenses; and one can say practical difference if the agent already returned the money to Reuven and afterward claims expenses — he can no longer acquire in the money since he already returned to Reuven; nevertheless if he wrote in it "all who are exempted," etc. — must return the expenses; and it is literally the case here:`,
  "13:ח": `As is written in Choshen Mishpat siman 264 — if one did good for his fellow and his fellow did not command him — nevertheless must pay him at least according to what he benefited; and specifically, etc. — permitted to accept wages — so Rambam? It is a mistake — rather they are Shulchan Gavoah's words; and the matter is not explicit in Shulchan Gavoah except the implication of his words thus according to Magen Avraham's understanding; and possibly meaning Rambam according to what Rambam wrote who gave reason there is no sachar Shabbat here since there is no melacha, etc.; and according to Maggid Mishneh as stated; and Magen Avraham's intent — all the time it is in others' hands whether they saved permissibly or in violation he can say I acquired for myself for my wages from hefker — for even if he did violation nevertheless he acquired it as Magen Avraham wrote above; but regarding l'chatchila there is a difference — where there is no violation he can l'chatchila save for himself from hefker for his wages; but where there is violation he is not permitted l'chatchila to save for his own need as stated; but b'dieved if he says I acquired for myself in violation he acquires; and all this while still in his hand; but if already returned — if there is violation even if the owners wish to give him the measure of his wages — we say forbidden for him to accept — for we are witnesses since he returned that his intent was not initially to save for his own need since there is violation — if he were saving for his own need it would be that all the time it was in his hand he would be believed that he did violation and saved for his own need since he was presumed; but when he returned — there are indications that he returned and also he is not presumed — we establish him in presumption of integrity until now that he did not do violation and did not save for himself; and now he turns back; but when he saved permissibly — meaning he cannot extract from owners in court since they are presumed — the owners can say from when did you save for yourself and there are indications he returned to me — presumptively he did not save for himself; nevertheless if the owners wish to give him willingly — permission is in his hand to accept; however in my humble opinion for us there is no practical difference whether he saved in violation — if he saved food or vessel to a courtyard that is not mixed — why said certainly he did not save for himself so he not do violation — and what difference between saving for himself or saving for the homeowner's need — for according to what we rule like the first Tanna of Ben Beteira even for the homeowner's need forbidden to save to a courtyard that is not mixed; and since either way he did violation — why not believe he did violation to save for himself; and according to Pri Megadim who clarified Rama — even to one that is not mixed — behold specifically in garments and manner of wearing — then there is no violation even if he saved for himself; and likewise holy writings permitted to save even to one that is not mixed also saving for himself — there is no violation — for behold the reason of permission in holy writings is not on account of monetary loss but on account of disgrace of holy writings; if so what difference whether saving for the homeowner's need or for his own need — in all cases there is reason to permit; therefore you will not find it except for poskim like Ben Beteira who even food permitted to save to one that is not mixed — on account of monetary loss the Sages permitted; if so when saving for the homeowner's need to one that is not mixed he did no violation — for this the Sages permitted; unlike when saving for himself — for regarding himself since he has not yet acquired it shevut was not permitted as Magen Avraham wrote above — and he did violation then — properly one can say we are witnesses he was in presumption of integrity and did not do violation to save for himself; therefore if he saved food or vessels not in the manner of wearing he did no violation to save for himself and certainly saved for the homeowner's need — for he did no violation the Sages permitted him; and therefore if he returned to owners even if owners wish to give him for his wages — we do not allow him; but for us there is no practical difference and Magen Avraham did not need to conceal his words so much; and also Terumat HaShulchan copied all Magen Avraham's words — it appears he holds the law is thus; and in my humble opinion it appears as I wrote — for us there is no practical difference:`,
  "13:ט": `And see Choshen Mishpat, etc. — the law of the kingdom, etc.; and Shach wrote there siman 357 — even though in such a case we do not say law of the kingdom is law and therefore Rama concluded and so practiced — therefore we rule ye'ush does not help:`,
  "13:י": `And it is possible, etc. — that there is despair — if so they cannot say from hefker we acquired; and if they take wages it is sachar Shabbat; nevertheless since he said to you in such a manner — there is no law of the kingdom; and all the more no custom applies since he declared hefker and gave as gift to rescuers; nevertheless practical difference if he did not say to you and he lives alone and no non-Jew or other Israelite lives there except him and the rescuer — in such a case even if he does not say to you one can say from hefker from owners' despair — in such a case there is no reason of Bach and no reason of Magen Avraham in s.k. 11; nevertheless from law of kingdom and custom ye'ush does not help:`,
  "14:א": `(s.k. 14) And some are lenient, etc. — likewise utensils of use — meaning spoon, knife and bowl — things that are not garments:`,
  "14:ב": `And so in Semag — food and vessels, etc. — not as Shulchan Gavoah wrote and left it as a difficulty — in daf 122 we learned where does one save — to the mixed courtyard Ben Beteira says even to one that is not mixed; and to there he takes out all his utensils of use and wears all he can to wear, etc.; and Semag wrote food and vessels one saves only to the mixed courtyard; and afterward wrote garments one saves even to a courtyard that is not mixed; and Shulchan Gavoah understood vessels mentioned in the beginning means even garments — therefore he challenged three difficulties: one — in the beginning it teaches vessels meaning garments one saves only to mixed; and afterward wrote garments one saves even to not mixed; two — what reason to distinguish and be more lenient in garments and vessels to save even to not mixed more than food; three — from Mishnah language it implies no difference between food and vessels and garments — for after dispute of first Tanna and Ben Beteira it teaches "and to there he takes out all his utensils of use" — implies from "and to there," etc., implies for each as he said — for first Tanna specifically to mixed and Ben Beteira even to not mixed; and also on this "and he wears all he can," etc. — implies all things equal regarding saving — until here Shulchan Gavoah; and Magen Avraham came to settle the explanation is not thus — rather what Semag wrote in the beginning food and vessels one saves only to mixed — what he wrote vessels means utensils of use; and in the end it teaches explicitly garments and clothing they are permitted even to not mixed — and it turns out Semag's words do not contradict each other; and what was challenged to Shulchan Gavoah there is no reason to distinguish — Magen Avraham also wrote the reason since it is manner of wearing, etc.; unlike food and utensils of use; and what he further wrote Mishnah language does not imply thus — Magen Avraham also wrote thus is Mishnah's intent — "and to there" means for each as he said — for first Tanna specifically to mixed and Ben Beteira even to not mixed — takes out all utensils of use meaning such as knife and bowl; and afterward teaches "and he wears all he can," etc. — this is a matter on its own and does not refer to the beginning — for this wearing permitted for all even to not mixed since it is manner of wearing; and Mishnah closed what is obvious since it is manner of wearing permitted, etc., even to not mixed; and it is possible even according to Rabbenu Chananel:`,
  "14:ג": `And it appears to me not in the manner, etc. — not as Bach wrote regarding a ring, etc. — meaning Bach challenged Ba'al HaTerumah who wrote permitted to place tefillin into a purse with money in order to save the money via the tefillin on account of fire (and this is what is mentioned in Shulchan Aruch seif 15 — only Ba'al HaTerumah mentioned only on account of fire and Rav Beit Yosef in Shulchan Aruch added or on account of thieves and robbers) — but forbidden to place a ring into a purse with money to save on account of fire since perhaps the ring is nullified relative to money which is more important; and Bach challenged — since in the beginning required to save money to place tefillin with it — perforce deals with a courtyard that is not mixed (or in a house where the fire is according to Bach who brought Magen Avraham s.k. 1) — for if to mixed courtyard (and house where there is no fire) why need tefillin — for even money alone permitted to save on account of fire and coercion according to those who permit in seif 1; and Ba'al HaTerumah is from those who permit; and since the beginning perforce deals with a courtyard that is not mixed — if so the end too placing a ring to the money also deals thus to a courtyard that is not mixed; and furthermore if for mixed also in the end difficult — why need a ring — behold money itself permitted according to Ba'al HaTerumah as stated; and since it deals with not mixed — behold even the ring itself not permitted to save except to mixed; and why say perhaps the ring is nullified relative to money; and one can say forever both beginning and end deal with a courtyard that is not mixed — and nevertheless permitted to save there a ring since a ring is like a garment — permitted like law of garments permitted even to not mixed; and on this Magen Avraham distinguished — what does a ring help — it has law of a garment — nevertheless since he places it into the purse with money inside and does not place it on his finger — it is not in the manner of wearing and forbidden to not mixed; and what Asher HaTerumah did not see, etc. — meaning to resolve Bach's difficulty on Sefer HaTerumah — Magen Avraham also wrote and did not see, etc. — brought in Rabbenu Yehuda this section — beginning of Ba'al HaTerumah's words to permit saving money from coercion and afterward appears from his words to permit also saving money from fire; and he concluded even if also forbidden on account of fire to save his money without any holy thing with them in a pouch lest bandits come to extinguish — but on account of rulers and fear of robbers of money permitted since there is not so much for d'oraisa prohibition, etc.; and Rabbenu Yehuda wrote on this, and this is his language: it appears from his words that to save on account of fire it is not straightforward to him thus to permit moving muktzeh object — end of his words; and even though afterward Rabbenu Yehuda wrote from Ran parsha One For Whom It Became Dark who wrote in name of Tosafos and Ba'al HaTerumah permitted to move money on account of bandits or fire — he was not so precise — for in name of Ba'al HaTerumah he wrote first to permit on account of coercion; but on account of fire he wrote in name of Tosafos — see there; and according to this Ba'al HaTerumah's words here are not clear — for also a ring forever both in beginning and end deals with mixed courtyard; and that tefillin and ring were needed to save plain money on account of fire was not clear to him; and in order the permission be clear he gave an enactment via tefillin; but a ring — no; and what Shulchan Aruch wrote this section to permit moving money also on account of fire — this is not Ba'al HaTerumah's view but Tosafos' view; however also Tur this section appears he understood from Ba'al HaTerumah's words that even on account of fire permitted to move money:`,
  "15:_": `(s.k. 15) Holy writings, etc. — save Written Torah before Oral Torah; and he concluded there in Sha'arei Chayim even Oral Torah borrowed or rented to him and if he does not save Oral Torah he must pay owners — nevertheless Written Torah first; so too in Sefer Ateret Rosheinu in name of Sha'arei Chayim siman 661:`,
  "16:_": `(s.k. 16) Nowadays for the heart has diminished — meaning from the aspect of law Written Torah is written only Ashurit and holy language and Oral Torah forbidden to write; and if in alteration they are Written Torah written not properly or if one wrote Oral Torah — one does not read in them and also does not save them on Shabbat on account of fire; but nowadays for the heart has diminished when intellect and memory diminished — permitted to write everything even Oral Torah; and likewise Written Torah as explained in Magen Avraham in the s.k. after who wrote "a time to act for the Lord, they have violated Your Torah" — therefore one reads in them and saves them on account of fire on Shabbat:`,
  "17:א": `(s.k. 17) Expert in it — but if not, etc. — as in Ran ch. 22 who proved from permitted regarding Megillah Gephtit for Gephtim and from inference all the more in books; and behold there in Megillah they permitted only Gephtit for Gephtim who understand Gephtit language; and also reasonable thus — for they permitted only on account of "a time to act"; and if they do not understand that language — what "a time to act" is there; however Ran concluded there: but when we permitted Gephtit for Gephtim and others — it is reasonable specifically for those who do not understand holy language; but for experts — no — end of his words; and it is not implied thus in Shulchan Aruch — it implies even if he knows holy language one saves if expert in that foreign language; and it is possible even though below siman 691 seif 10 regarding Megillah he ruled thus — if he understands holy language he does not fulfill with foreign language — nevertheless since Rashi's view also in Megillah even if he understands holy language he fulfills with foreign language he understands as explained there in Beit Yosef and he did not rule like Rashi regarding Megillah since many poskim disagree — nevertheless regarding saving he was more lenient since according to R' Chiya in gemara ch. 22 even in what one does not read — nevertheless one saves on account of disgrace of holy writings even though we do not rule like R' Chiya — nevertheless in this case he was lenient:`,
  "17:ב": `And Rambam wrote, etc. — he brought Rambam's words to learn from him that by law as there is concern we require specifically holy language — likewise by law we require specifically Ashurit script in order to be lenient afterward:`,
  "17:ג": `And requires further study — for although they permitted, etc. — why permit in another script; for here there is no "a time to act" — for all Israel are expert in Ashurit script; on the contrary more expert than another script:`,
  "17:ד": `But requires further study on what basis they relied, etc. — in another script, etc. — meaning they write holy language in another script — meaning meshita (and it is our script for writing a letter) or what is called Rashi script similar to Ashurit since its root is Ashurit only changed:`,
  "17:ה": `And also in printing it would appear to forbid — meaning holy language in another script:`,
  "17:ו": `For not everyone is expert to write, etc. — if so this too is in the category of "a time to act" — for if written Ashurit not everyone knows to read it but will not always find one who knows to write it:`,
  "17:ז": `And requires further study — for behold, etc. — and who permitted them — meaning specifically among Israel there is on account of "a time to act" so all Israel can learn and Torah not be forgotten from Israel; but among non-Jews — what if they do not learn — therefore among them there is no "a time to act":`,
  "17:ח": `And Rama in responsum wrote, etc. — and one can say specifically in Ashurit script they were not given to write, etc.; and see Yoreh De'ah, etc. in Beit Yosef who wrote another reason from what Rama wrote in responsum; nevertheless implied from Rama's words in responsum that meshita script is not script and there is no prohibition writing in meshita Oral Torah matters — if so there is to resolve what he left above in requires further study on what basis they relied, etc. in script that is not Ashurit thus — Magen Avraham's intent; but it does not refer to Shulchan Gavoah's view who wrote gentiles' writing certainly counts as writing as Magen Avraham wrote siman 307 s.k. 10; and so wrote in Beit Shmuel in Even HaEzer section 126 — rather suffices to resolve they practiced writing twenty-four books in holy language in meshita script — as forbidden for Oral Torah matters one may not write them — nevertheless meshita is not writing — likewise not called writing for prohibition of writing twenty-four books in holy language and meshita script:`,
  "17:ט": `And nevertheless requires further study — for behold even in Talmudic times, etc. — Gephtit for Gephtim, etc. — if so difficult on Shulchan Aruch who wrote or in another language the people understand, etc. — this was not needed on account of "a time to act" to permit in our time — for likewise also by law in Talmudic times they read in them and saved; and also on Shulchan Gavoah one can question somewhat on condition he was in doubt in books written in their language — since they understand that language by Talmudic law permitted and one reads in them — they have sanctity:`,
  "17:י": `And in Beit Shmuel, etc. — in this there is somewhat resolution to his first difficulty on Shulchan Gavoah and "who permitted them"; and according to Beit Shmuel's words even among non-Jews there is somewhat "a time to act" so they see the comfort of Israel:`,
  "17:כ": `In Mordekhai, etc. — to copy a Chumash book in their language — as it should read:`,
  "18:א": `(s.k. 18) Ashurit, etc. — this is, etc.; and also Tosafos so too; and it is difficult, etc. — need to present briefly Tosafos' words — in first chapter of Megillah the Sages and Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel dispute — Sages hold books are written in every language; Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel requires specifically holy language except sefer Torah permitted to write also in Greek and not another language; and we learned ch. 22 all holy writings one saves from fire; and they dispute if written in another language that is not holy language — if one saves it from fire; and for Sages who hold written in every language and read in them — straightforward one saves; only for Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel who holds in other languages not written and if written in another language not read in them — if nevertheless one saves from fire — R' Huna said one does not save since one does not read in them; and Rav Chisda said one saves on account of disgrace of holy writings; and they conclude there tannaim dispute in dispute of R' Huna and R' Chisda; and they say there the Exilarch asked Rabba bar R' Huna — if written in holy language in ink and red paint — if one saves; and the question was asked both according to R' Huna and R' Chisda — for R' Huna although he holds if written in another language one does not save — nevertheless if written in ink and red paint since written in holy language it is preferable and one saves; or according to R' Chisda who holds if written in another language one saves — nevertheless if written in ink and red paint even written in holy language it is inferior and he agrees one does not save; and Rav Ashi brought a braita from which we hear one saves — for it teaches there is no difference between books and Megillah except books are written in every language and Megillah until written Ashurit on parchment and in ink — implies books do not require Ashurit and ink and one saves; and Tosafos there daf 115b s.v. Megillah, etc. — they challenged — proven from tractate Megillah that also other books require parchment and ink; therefore Tosafos ruled this braita regarding saving was said — other books even written in other languages and not on parchment and ink — nevertheless one saves; but Megillah one saves only in Ashurit writing on parchment and ink; and reason to distinguish between them — since also books require parchment and ink like Megillah — why in other books even written not on parchment and ink nevertheless one saves and not Megillah; and they resolved — books are different on account of Divine Names in them one saves; and wrote in Sefer HaTerumah; and you should challenge from braita here against what was said above "wherever not given to read one does not save" (meaning books one does not read if not written on parchment and ink and nevertheless one saves; and even though in this the Exilarch was in doubt if written in ink and red paint preferable — R' Huna agrees one saves if written in holy language — for superiority of language causes permission to save; but in braita that teaches regarding saving other books written in every language not on parchment and not in ink — you should challenge it comes according to Sages against Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel — books if written in other languages one reads in them — nevertheless if written in other languages and ink and red paint — in any case there is no superiority of holy language and since one does not read — if so according to R' Huna one does not save; and behold braita teaches one saves) — and one can say this concludes above it is a tannaitic dispute — end of Tosafos; and according to R' Huna whom we rule like him — if written in ink and red paint (even written in holy language) according to him the question is not resolved — for this braita holds not like R' Huna; and in every case where one does not read nevertheless one saves; if so R' Huna who holds like the other tanna who thus one does not read one does not save — the Exilarch's doubt remains if written in holy language and ink and red paint if one saves; and doubt fitting to be stringent; and so in Chiddushei Rashba — for Tosafos the question is not resolved according to R' Huna:`,
  "18:ב": `Nevertheless Mordekhai's view, etc.; and Rabbenu Yehuda ruled like Mordekhai; and since in any case written in holy language and combined that it has Divine Names:`,
  "18:ג": `And nevertheless requires further study, etc. — for the law is thus according to Talmudic times; but nowadays, etc.:`,
  "18:ד": `And Rabbenu Yehuda did not examine so much — who wrote in Shulchan Aruch Megillah one saves only if written Ashurit on parchment and ink — and this means nowadays even written in other languages and not on parchment — only if written in ink one saves it:`,
  "18:ה": `And what is written in seif 2, etc. — in ink and red paint, etc. — behold according to Magen Avraham's words writing in ink and red paint here "a time to act" does not apply; and so reasonable — if so one does not read — if so likewise one does not save; where written in every language and ink and red paint — for us this is preferable as Magen Avraham wrote in the s.k. before that — it is possible when it is another language permitted to write also in another script — end of his words; and likewise permitted in ink and red paint:`,
  "19:_": `(s.k. 19) And some say one saves. And Bach and Maharshal ruled like the first view; also Rabbenu Yehuda's view is thus as is his way in most places he adopts the first view as primary; and so in Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 179 seif 12, and this is his language: permitted to heal with an amulet, etc., that has verses in them, etc.; but to write verses in an amulet forbidden — end of his words; and the reason it is like burning them lest fire fall on Shabbat and he will not be permitted to save them — for in amulet there is no "a time to act" — and this is like the first view:`,
  "20:א": `(s.k. 20) And some say — and it appears to me there is no dispute here — meaning not as Rabbenu Yehuda understood that there is dispute among poskim he brought in name of Mordekhai who wrote forbidden to place tefillin with money on Shabbat day even to save them from fire and from thieves; but from Semag's language it appears even if tefillin were placed on Shabbat permitted — for Tur wrote, and this is his language: Ba'al HaTerumah wrote if he placed tefillin in a purse full of money he can save it like law of tefillin; and since he did not write if he placed them erev Shabbat — implies even if placed on Shabbat permitted to move the money — not like Mordekhai; and therefore Shulchan Aruch wrote two views; and on this Magen Avraham wrote there is no dispute between them as will be explained:`,
  "20:ב": `To save the money (to a courtyard that is not mixed, etc.; and Bach did not understand thus, etc. — they contradict each other) — what is printed in two half-circles is a separate matter as is customary; and Magen Avraham comes to resolve Bach's difficulties according to Rabbenu Yehuda's understanding that there is dispute among poskim whether permitted to place tefillin with money on Shabbat — according to this Bach challenged the two seifim contradict each other — in this seif he brought two views whether permitted to place them on Shabbat; and in the seif after he wrote some permit to save, etc., via loaf and child, etc.; and since language "some permit" is because some say they did not permit via loaf and child except for a corpse alone — and certainly placing loaf or child is on Shabbat like holy objects mentioned by a corpse — deals on Shabbat; if so in any case there is dispute whether permitted to do thus on Shabbat via placing tefillin — all the more via placing loaf and child on Shabbat — forbidden for those who forbid with tefillin; and for this Magen Avraham explained — in this seif deals with wanting to save money via tefillin like law of tefillin to a courtyard that is not mixed — in this poskim dispute whether permitted to place on Shabbat; but in the seif after deals with wanting to save to reshus hayachid or mixed courtyard only the money is muktzeh — therefore wanted to move via loaf and child — in this all agree even to place on Shabbat permitted except there are disputants who did not say loaf, etc., except for a corpse alone:`,
  "20:ג": `For behold it is in the gemara (daf 117a) that by law was, etc.; and as is written end siman 310 — even though we rule if forbidden object and permitted object placed together permitted to move the vessel that is basis for forbidden and also for permitted; nevertheless if possible to shake the forbidden from the vessel one must shake; and therefore Mordekhai wrote one should place them erev Shabbat — comes to exclude not to place them on Shabbat at time of fire; but agrees if placed there before fire and afterward fire fell — permitted to save money via tefillin; and also those who permit do not permit to place on Shabbat at time of fire; and therefore Tur was unspecified in name of Ba'al HaTerumah and wrote if he placed tefillin, etc.; and did not write if he placed tefillin erev Shabbat — for in truth even if placed on Shabbat permitted; only behold forbidden to place at time of fire; but placed on Shabbat not at time of fire — permitted to save via them also the money; and this Mordekhai also agrees permitted and there is no dispute here:`,
  "23:_": `(s.k. 23) That it is permitted to say — for shevut of amira, etc. — meaning even though we do not rule like R' Shimon in Eruvin 97b who said one who desecrates shevut stands before holy writings; and as is stated below siman 352 — meaning Israel should do shevut; but shevut of amira which is lighter — permitted on account of disgrace of holy writings:`,
  "24:א": `(s.k. 24) The margins cut, etc. — for if not cut — must say saved via the book:`,
  "24:ב": `Or erased, etc. — erased straightforward to him the gemara one does not save — initially this place was not sanctified except via the letters; and since letters erased — also sanctity of margin written on it goes; except margins from the side and similar that those margins were sanctified on their own — in this the gemara is in doubt if cut whether saved or not and not resolved; therefore suffices one does not save:`,
  "24:ג": `And if so one does not save. Meaning in it there is no doubt but certainly one does not save — for certainly not sanctified:`,
  "24:ד": `In new ones permitted, etc. — possibly in new ones there is heart of court stipulation on them — for in any case need to bind and cut margins to equal pages; but old ones already cut once — to cut again in this possibly no heart of court stipulation; but custom now also cuts old ones — must say heart of court stipulates each time need arises to cut what was need of the book — at minimum one should be careful in old books where they glue in torn place not to glue on words especially Divine Names:`,
  "24:ה": `And Taz wrote s.k. 16 in name of Bach those coins stamped with the four-letter Name heretics stamped for idolatry — forbidden to keep in one's possession rather melt immediately; and Taz added what is practiced in some communities when blessing sick and volunteering money for the sick placing money inside aron kodesh — there is additional prohibition forbidden to place mundane matter into aron kodesh which is lowering from sanctity — see there what he wrote regarding lowering from sanctity; see Magen Avraham siman 154 s.k. 14; and what he wrote in name of Bach regarding coins stamped with four-letter Name — wrote in responsum Chavot Yair siman 16 (page 26a), and this is his language: and according to this in coins stamped in lands of Sweden with four-letter Name there was doubt; nevertheless it appears to me they are permitted since stamped to circulate them and it is like writing the Name for mundane purpose — end of his words; behold opposite Bach's words; and perhaps there is to distinguish between coins stamped by kingdom about which Chavot Yair spoke in that responsum — and Bach deals with coins made by craftsman smith for private non-Jew; and therefore Bach wrote heretics stamped for idolatry; and also perhaps distinguish between unspecified non-Jew and heretic; and see Yoreh De'ah siman 481:`,
  "25:_": `(s.k. 25) That he not intend — meaning not, etc. Bach not like Beit Yosef — for in Tur he wrote he is not concerned if it extinguishes (and therefore permitted) since he does not intend for that; and Rabbenu Yehuda explained specifically if his intent without extinguishing is to cover and read in the book and his mind is not at all that it extinguish — then permitted; but if his mind that via covering with cloak and reading in book it will extinguish — forbidden; but Bach disagrees necessarily — see there — even if his mind is not on wearing or reading but only that it extinguish — also permitted; and forbidden only that he not shake or run and jump, etc. — for these acts cause extinguishing:`,
  "27:_": `(s.k. 27) Non-Jew who comes, etc. — but, etc. — since the non-Jew intends for benefit — meaning for the Israelite's benefit; and also the Israelite's body benefits — therefore forbidden:`,
};
